meta-custom

Summary

meta-custom is a custom Yocto layer for Raspberry Pi 4 (64-bit) that integrates a Python-based face-recognition and attendance application.

The application uses OpenCV for face detection and recognition, captures images through a USB webcam, records attendance, and sends email notifications using Gmail SMTP.

The layer includes:

Face-recognition application (main.py).

Known face images and Haar Cascade model.

Email configuration through a .env file.

Required Python, OpenCV and system runtime dependencies.

USB webcam support through the Linux UVC kernel module.

Custom Yocto image (custom-image).

Installation Guide

1. Clone the repository

Clone this layer into your Yocto project directory:

git clone https://github.com/singaravela45/meta-custom.git

2. Add the required Yocto layers

Ensure the following layers are available:

meta-raspberrypi

meta-openembedded/meta-oe

meta-openembedded/meta-python

Add the custom layer to conf/bblayers.conf:

BBLAYERS += " \
    /path/to/yocto/meta-custom \
"

3. Configure email credentials

Create the following file:

meta-custom/recipes-core/allfiles/files/.env

Add your email configuration:

SENDER_EMAIL=your_sender@gmail.com
RECEIVER_EMAIL=your_receiver@gmail.com
GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD=your_gmail_app_password

Important:

Replace all three values with your own credentials.

The .env file is included in the generated image, so protect the image and target device.

4. Build the Yocto image

Initialize the Yocto build environment:

source poky/oe-init-build-env build

Build the custom image:

bitbake custom-image

The generated image will be available under:

tmp/deploy/images/raspberrypi4-64/

5. Flash and run

Flash the generated image onto the Raspberry Pi 4 SD card and boot the board.

Connect a compatible USB webcam.

Run the application:

cd /home/root/files
python3 main.py

The application loads the known face images, trains the recognizer, initializes the webcam, and starts face recognition.